1
00:00:00,300 --> 00:00:08,081
Princeton, New Jersey. April, nineteen fifty-five. An old man in a hospital bed asks for only one thing.

2
00:00:08,431 --> 00:00:12,018
EINSTEIN: My papers, please. And my glasses. I am not finished.

3
00:00:12,318 --> 00:00:14,230
DOCTOR: Professor Einstein, you need to rest.

4
00:00:14,530 --> 00:00:18,548
EINSTEIN: Rest? I have rested enough. There is still one equation missing.

5
00:00:18,898 --> 00:00:24,895
That's him. And you've known him for fifty years. To see how, we go back to a newspaper ad.

6
00:00:27,095 --> 00:00:33,507
Bern, Switzerland. Nineteen-oh-two. You're a broke young student, and a tiny ad catches your eye.

7
00:00:33,807 --> 00:00:39,385
YOU: Private lessons in mathematics and physics. Trial lesson free. Albert Einstein. Free? I'll take it.

8
00:00:39,735 --> 00:00:45,748
A young man with wild dark hair opens the door. He's twenty-three, and he has no job.

9
00:00:46,048 --> 00:00:51,070
EINSTEIN: Come in, come in! You want to learn physics? Good. Then tell me first: what is time?

10
00:00:51,370 --> 00:00:53,296
YOU: Time? It's, um, what clocks measure?

11
00:00:53,546 --> 00:00:58,045
EINSTEIN: Ha! And what do clocks measure? Time! You see? We are already in trouble.

12
00:00:58,345 --> 00:00:59,925
YOU: So what is time, really?

13
00:01:00,175 --> 00:01:02,769
EINSTEIN: I don't know yet. That is the fun part!

14
00:01:03,119 --> 00:01:07,812
The trial lesson lasts four hours. He never asks you for a single franc.

15
00:01:08,112 --> 00:01:12,541
EINSTEIN: Forget the lessons. Come back tomorrow. Talking is better than teaching.

16
00:01:14,741 --> 00:01:22,003
Soon you're part of a tiny club with his friends, Maurice Solovine and Conrad Habicht. They give it a grand name.

17
00:01:22,303 --> 00:01:28,436
HABICHT: I hereby open the session of the Olympia Academy! Tonight's feast: one sausage, some cheese, and tea.

18
00:01:28,736 --> 00:01:31,500
SOLOVINE: And tonight's book is David Hume. Albert, you start.

19
00:01:31,750 --> 00:01:37,978
EINSTEIN: Hume says we never see a cause. We see one thing, and then another. So, can we be sure of anything?

20
00:01:38,278 --> 00:01:40,215
YOU: We can be sure the sausage is gone.

21
00:01:40,565 --> 00:01:43,691
HABICHT: Psst. For his birthday, we bought real caviar. Watch this.

22
00:01:43,991 --> 00:01:50,230
He's busy explaining Galileo's law of inertia. He eats the whole plate, and never notices.

23
00:01:50,530 --> 00:01:55,293
EINSTEIN: So that was caviar? Well. Fine food is wasted on a peasant like me.

24
00:01:55,643 --> 00:02:08,247
He needs money, so a friend helps him get a job: Technical Expert, Third Class, at the Swiss Patent Office. Soon after, he marries Mileva Marić, a physics student from his university.

25
00:02:08,547 --> 00:02:12,124
MILEVA: Albert, you'll be reading patents for electric clocks all day.

26
00:02:12,374 --> 00:02:18,801
EINSTEIN: Exactly, Mileva! Clocks! How do you make two clocks in two cities agree? That is a deep question.

27
00:02:21,001 --> 00:02:28,216
Nineteen-oh-five. A warm evening in May. He drags you and his best friend, Michele Besso, to a café.

28
00:02:28,516 --> 00:02:31,652
EINSTEIN: Besso, you must help me. I think I am going mad.

29
00:02:31,902 --> 00:02:34,506
BESSO: Calm down, Albert. Which problem is it this time?

30
00:02:34,756 --> 00:02:42,050
EINSTEIN: Light! If I chase a beam of light, does it slow down for me? It cannot. Light always moves at the same speed.

31
00:02:42,350 --> 00:02:44,203
BESSO: Then something else must give way.

32
00:02:44,453 --> 00:02:46,390
EINSTEIN: But what? Space? Time?

33
00:02:46,740 --> 00:02:51,475
The next morning, at the patent office, he walks straight up to Besso. No hello.

34
00:02:51,775 --> 00:02:54,375
EINSTEIN: Thank you. I've completely solved the problem.

35
00:02:54,675 --> 00:02:59,863
EINSTEIN: Time is not the same for everyone. Two clocks, moving differently, do not agree.

36
00:03:00,163 --> 00:03:02,220
YOU: So my now and your now aren't the same?

37
00:03:02,470 --> 00:03:05,001
EINSTEIN: Not if we are moving. Isn't it wonderful?

38
00:03:05,351 --> 00:03:07,942
YOU: Albert, how many papers did you send out this year?

39
00:03:08,192 --> 00:03:13,572
EINSTEIN: Five! Light, atoms, relativity, and my doctorate. Not bad for a clerk, no?

40
00:03:13,922 --> 00:03:22,971
That year, a twenty-six-year-old patent clerk publishes papers on light, on atoms, and on relativity. They call it his miracle year.

41
00:03:25,171 --> 00:03:27,867
YOU: You wrote one more paper? It's only three pages!

42
00:03:28,117 --> 00:03:33,206
EINSTEIN: Short, but important. Mass and energy are the same thing, in different clothes.

43
00:03:33,506 --> 00:03:35,945
YOU: So how much energy is in, say, one raisin?

44
00:03:36,195 --> 00:03:41,775
EINSTEIN: Take its mass. Multiply by the speed of light, squared. A very, very big number.

45
00:03:42,125 --> 00:03:54,572
E equals m c squared. One gram of matter holds the energy of about twenty thousand tons of explosives. In nineteen-oh-five, nobody imagines where this leads.

46
00:03:56,772 --> 00:04:01,481
Nineteen-oh-seven. He's still at the patent office, staring out of the window.

47
00:04:01,781 --> 00:04:04,588
EINSTEIN: Ach! I just had the happiest thought of my life.

48
00:04:04,838 --> 00:04:06,519
YOU: They finally promoted you?

49
00:04:06,769 --> 00:04:10,753
EINSTEIN: Better! A man falling from a roof does not feel his own weight.

50
00:04:11,003 --> 00:04:12,463
YOU: Until he hits the ground.

51
00:04:12,763 --> 00:04:20,396
EINSTEIN: Before that! Falling and floating are the same. So gravity is not a pull at all. It is space and time, bending.

52
00:04:20,746 --> 00:04:28,237
Turning that one thought into mathematics takes him years. By nineteen twelve, he begs an old classmate for help.

53
00:04:28,537 --> 00:04:31,580
EINSTEIN: Grossmann, you must help me, or I'll go crazy!

54
00:04:31,830 --> 00:04:36,515
GROSSMANN: Riemann's geometry? Albert, it's a terrible mess. Physicists should stay away from it.

55
00:04:36,765 --> 00:04:38,958
EINSTEIN: Then I will make a mess, my friend.

56
00:04:41,158 --> 00:04:49,140
November, nineteen fifteen. Berlin. He tests his finished theory on an old puzzle: the strange orbit of Mercury.

57
00:04:49,440 --> 00:04:54,626
EINSTEIN: It fits. Exactly! My heart is racing. I am beside myself with joy!

58
00:04:54,976 --> 00:05:02,844
EINSTEIN: But the real test needs the Sun. If I'm right, starlight bends around it. We can only see that during a total eclipse.

59
00:05:03,144 --> 00:05:10,614
May, nineteen nineteen. The British astronomer Arthur Eddington photographs the eclipse from an island off Africa.

60
00:05:10,914 --> 00:05:14,579
EDDINGTON: The stars have shifted. Just as Einstein predicted.

61
00:05:14,929 --> 00:05:18,642
YOU: Professor, they confirmed it! But what if the photos had proved you wrong?

62
00:05:18,892 --> 00:05:22,944
EINSTEIN: Then I would have felt sorry for the dear Lord. The theory is correct.

63
00:05:23,294 --> 00:05:29,706
Overnight, he's the most famous scientist on Earth. Newspapers call it a revolution in science.

64
00:05:30,056 --> 00:05:32,765
YOU: And now the Nobel Prize! For relativity, right?

65
00:05:33,015 --> 00:05:39,300
EINSTEIN: No! For my little paper on light, from nineteen-oh-five. They still don't trust relativity.

66
00:05:41,500 --> 00:05:49,220
Brussels, nineteen twenty-seven. The greatest physicists alive meet to argue about the strange rules of atoms.

67
00:05:49,520 --> 00:05:54,263
BOHR: At the smallest scales, nature is a game of chance. We can only know probabilities.

68
00:05:54,513 --> 00:05:57,780
EINSTEIN: I cannot believe that. God does not play dice.

69
00:05:58,030 --> 00:06:00,429
BOHR: Einstein, stop telling God what to do!

70
00:06:00,779 --> 00:06:08,057
At every meeting, he brings a new thought experiment to break the theory. And every time, Bohr finds an answer.

71
00:06:08,357 --> 00:06:14,598
EINSTEIN: Bohr! A box full of light, with a clock inside. Weigh the box, and your uncertainty is broken!

72
00:06:14,848 --> 00:06:20,334
BOHR: Ah, but a clock that moves in gravity runs differently, Albert. Your own relativity saves me!

73
00:06:20,634 --> 00:06:21,854
YOU: So who's winning?

74
00:06:22,104 --> 00:06:24,758
EINSTEIN: Bohr, today. Tomorrow? We shall see.

75
00:06:25,058 --> 00:06:34,739
Experiments mostly side with Bohr. But Einstein's objections lead to quantum entanglement, the idea behind today's quantum computers.

76
00:06:36,939 --> 00:06:43,636
Nineteen thirty-three. Hitler takes power. Einstein is visiting America, and he never goes back.

77
00:06:43,936 --> 00:06:48,007
EINSTEIN: I will not live in a country where people are not equal before the law.

78
00:06:48,357 --> 00:06:55,991
The Nazis seize his home and burn his books. He settles in Princeton. Six years later, a visitor brings a warning.

79
00:06:56,291 --> 00:07:01,962
SZILARD: German scientists have split the uranium atom. A chain reaction could make a bomb of enormous power.

80
00:07:02,212 --> 00:07:04,007
EINSTEIN: I never even thought of that.

81
00:07:04,307 --> 00:07:05,492
YOU: What will you do?

82
00:07:05,742 --> 00:07:09,746
EINSTEIN: Then we must warn the President. Before Hitler gets there first.

83
00:07:10,096 --> 00:07:16,395
His letter to Roosevelt helps start the atomic bomb project. Einstein never works on it himself.

84
00:07:16,695 --> 00:07:24,497
EINSTEIN: I made one great mistake in my life, when I signed that letter. But there was the danger that the Germans would make it first.

85
00:07:26,697 --> 00:07:37,317
Back to that hospital bed. April eighteenth, nineteen fifty-five. He dies in his sleep, at seventy-six. His last calculations lie beside him.

86
00:07:37,667 --> 00:07:40,083
YOU: He never found the equation he was looking for.

87
00:07:40,383 --> 00:07:49,314
But the GPS in your phone corrects for relativity every day. Without it, your position would drift by about ten kilometers a day.

88
00:07:49,614 --> 00:07:55,941
Lasers, solar panels, even the first photo of a black hole, all trace back to his ideas.

89
00:07:56,241 --> 00:07:58,928
YOU: Professor, what should I tell my own students one day?

90
00:07:59,178 --> 00:08:05,983
EINSTEIN: The important thing is not to stop questioning. I have no special talents. I am only passionately curious.

91
00:08:06,333 --> 00:08:11,935
So, if you were his student, which question would you chase for fifty years? Tell me in the comments.
